 that the display  scrollsup with more of the text file becoming visible on the bottom line. T                                             There are many ways of moving the  cursor  around  the  text  file.  Bypressing the CRSR keys on the bottom left hand side of the keyboard  the  cursorwill be made to move. To reverse the direction of the curs	       At the moment you are probably reading this in the 80 column  mode.  Toswitch to the 40 column mode hold down the CTRL key and press the letter O. Thiscommand switches between the display modes. Try it now. The 40  column  mode  ismore easy to rea pressedagain. Another useful key is the CLR HOME key. When  this  key  is  pressed  thecursor moves to the top line of the display. Try this key now and then  use  thecursor keys to move the cursor back onto this line. Notice that the current lineand co
l up the help pages at any time by pressing the grey f3 key.This displays one of the help pages which contains a summary of all the  commandkeys available. Subsequent pressings of  f3  causes  another  help  page  to  bedisplayed, there are three help pa␀mmand moves text to the right of the cursor  onespace to the right. Use these commands to move the line below left and right.                                                                                                             <<< CTRL Q press CTR                                         The next key to learn about is the tab stop key. This is  the  leftwardpointing arrow on the top left hand side of the keyboard. When  you  press  thiskey the cursor jumps to the next tab column indicated by the rand press CTRL DEL. Try deleting the word  'centronics'  from  theline below using the word delete command;                                                                                                                               Tasword 64 can use c             The RETURN key when pressed causes the cursor to drop down a  line  andmove to the left hand side of the display. Try using the RETURN key to move  thedisplay up.                                                                               efore. Then you must press CTRL I. Forpractice try inserting a blank line before the line below;                                                                                                      ***** Move the cursor to the left hand side and press CTu must place the cursor on the unwanted  line  and  thenhold down the CTRL key and press the  CLR  HOME  key.  This  is  abbreviated  toCTRL CLR. Try deleting the line below;                                                                                 INST  andextra spaces will be inserted in the line. If the line is already full  then  nospaces can be inserted. If this is the case then move the cursor to  before  theword you wish to insert text before. Now press CTRL I. You  will  see  that  therestion  the  cursor  after  theletter you wish to delete and press the DEL key. The letters  after  the  cursorwill now move to the left one space. Try to delete the extra '*' from Tasword onthe line below;                                                                                                                                                                                                         The CTRL Q command moves the text that is to the left of the cursor  onespace to the left. The CTRL E coges altogether. At any  time  whilst  you  arereading the help page you can press RETURN to get back to  the  text  file.  Tryusing the f3 key now to call the help pages.                                                                                            Alternatively you can use the SHIFT CLR delete. This  behaves  like  theDEL command except the cursor remains stationary. Try using this command on  theabove practice line. To delete a word from a line position the cursor  over  theunwanted word aised marks on theline above the status line. Try pressing this key now and observe  what  happensto the cursor.                                                                                                                                              entronics or Commodore printers.                                                                                                            If you wish to insert a blank line then move the cursor to the left handside of the line you wish to insert text b                                                                               Now that you have seen how some of the cursor controls work it  is  nowtime to see how the various commands work for manipulating  the  text  file.  Todelete a line of text yoRL I *****                                                                                                      If you wish to insert spaces between words or letters then position  thecursor on the letter you wish to insert spaces before. Now press SHIFT                                           ****  THIS is A DEMONSTARTION of how to DELETE a LINE from the text file  ****                                                                                          To delete a letter from a word simply posit	 of the line after the cursor is moved onto a new line leaving you space  totype in your extra text. Try using the SHIFT INST and CTRL  I  commands  on  thepractice line above. When you have finished use the CTRL CLR command  to  deletethe practice line.                                                                                                                  Tasw*ord 64  by Tasman Software Ltd.                                                                                                        
L E >>>                                                                                                                   If you wish to centre this line in the  middle  of  the  text  move  thecursor onto the line you wish to be centred and press CTRL W␀L G switches Word Wrapon or off. 'Insert' mode on causes a blank line to be inserted  every  time  youtype a full line of text or press RETURN. Insert mode is turned on or off  usingthe CTRL H  command.  This  enables  you  to  type  in  new  paragraphs  pressCTRL [ . To move to the right hand margin press CTRL ] . Try these keys now.                                                                                            Two more useful cursor  commands  are  the  word  left  and  word  rightcommands                                                                                                                               **** Try ConVeRtinG thiS LiNe To EiThEr UpPeR Or LoWeR CaSe ****                                                                                                                                 You may of noticed that the tutor file has been  right  justified.  Thismeans that extra spaces have been inserted between words so  that  the  all  thelines are parallel down the right  hd. This willset a tab stop if one doesn't exist there or it will remove one if a tab stop isalready there. Pressing CTRL Z removes all the tab stops and CTRL X  resets  thetab stops to their default positions of one every ten  characters.  To  set  thema                                           **** This line can be justified or unjustified ****                                                                                                        To unjustify,that is remove the surplus spaces,a line  mments using the keys mentioned in this paragraph. When you  havefinished press CTRL S to reset the margins to 1 and 80.                                                                                                                  You will notice that           When typing in text you may wish to type a section in upper  case  only.If this is the case you can either  hold  down  the  SHIFT  key  and  press  therequired letter or alternatively you can press the f5 function key to  put  capslock on. Preor off. Pressing CTRL F turns it  onor off. The 'W/W' message tells you whether word wrap is on or  off.  Word  wrapallows you to type without having to look at the screen. If a word  overflows  aline then the entire word is moved onto the next line. CTR. Try  this  now  onthe practice line above.                                                                                                                                                A quick way of moving the cursor to the left hand  margin  is  to sor on the required line and hold down CTRL + and  thecursor will move through the text converting lower to upper case. The reverse ofthis is CTRL - which converts upper case letters  to  lower  case  letters.  Trythese commands on the line below;       . These are CTRL < and CTRL  >  keys  respectively.  These  advance  thecursor to the next word or back to the previous word. Try using  these  commandsnow to skip through this paragraph.                                                                                                     Now you will learn how you can set the margins and tab  stops  to   yourown settings. To set a tab stop move the cursor to the required column and pressCTRL and the left arrow key on the top left hand side of the keyboarand  margin.  To  justify  a  line  yourselfposition the cursor on the line to be justified and press CTRL K. Try  this  nowon the line below;                                                                                                                rgins move the cursor to the required column and press CTRL A to set the  lefthand margin and CTRL D to set the right hand margin. Pressing CTRL S resets  thema